# Copy to secrets.env and fill in. secrets.env is gitignored — never commit it.
#
#   cp configs/secrets.env.example configs/secrets.env
#   set -a; source configs/secrets.env; set +a

# --- AI filtering -----------------------------------------------------------
# https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...
# Decision model that names the page when a search comes back empty (a wall
# or an empty city?). Any model on OpenRouter's Decisions API; leave unset for
# the default, set empty to turn the check off. Same key as above.
# RS_PAGE_CHECK_MODEL=openai/gpt-6-luna-decisions
# Decision model that screens a listing before the LLM reads it. Shadow by
# default: asked and logged, nothing skipped. Leave unset for the default,
# set empty to turn it off. ENFORCE=1 lets a reject at or above THRESHOLD
# skip the LLM; flipping it here and recreating the workers is the whole
# change, no release. The homelab workers read this file through env_file.
# RS_PRESCREEN_MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.14
# RS_PRESCREEN_ENFORCE=0
# RS_PRESCREEN_THRESHOLD=0.99

# --- Coordinator storage ----------------------------------------------------
# Where challenge screenshots are written. Unset, they go to a screenshots/
# directory beside the database file; the containers set /data/screenshots so
# they live on the coordinator volume and survive a redeploy.
# RS_SCREENSHOT_DIR=/data/screenshots

# --- Telegram alerts --------------------------------------------------------
# Create a bot with @BotFather, then get the chat id by messaging the bot and
# visiting https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
TELEGRAM_TOKEN=123456789:AA...
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=-1001234567890

# Priority topics: listing alerts are grouped into forum topics by the AI's
# counted units — 🔥 high (50+), ⚡ medium (10-49), 🐢 low (5-9) — which show
# as a sidebar in the Telegram group. Requirements: the chat above must be a
# group with Topics enabled (group Settings -> Topics) and the bot must be an
# admin with "Manage topics". Then create the three topics and fill the ids in
# one go with:
#
#   scripts/setup-telegram-topics.py
#
# Any id left unset (and any listing whose count the AI could not pin) falls
# back to the group's General topic — never dropped.
# TELEGRAM_TOPIC_HIGH=
# TELEGRAM_TOPIC_MEDIUM=
# TELEGRAM_TOPIC_LOW=

# --- Facebook authentication ------------------------------------------------
# There are no Facebook passwords here. Workers authenticate from a saved
# browser session created once per account:
#
#   rs-session capture --storage-state configs/sessions/denver-1.json
#
# or, from cookies exported in a browser you are already logged into:
#
#   rs-session import --cookies cookies.txt \
#       --user-agent "<that browser's user agent>" \
#       --storage-state configs/sessions/denver-1.json
#
# Sessions live under configs/sessions/ (gitignored) and are mounted into each
# worker. Check them with `rs-session check`; they last on the order of weeks,
# so refresh before they lapse.
#
# Use a dedicated account per worker, never the company page account.

# --- Facebook two-factor codes ----------------------------------------------
# Every worker account has 2FA on, so every capture needs a six-digit code.
# Normally you do NOT set anything here: store each account's TOTP secret in its
# session sidecar once, and `rs-session capture` fills the code in by itself.
#
#   rs-session set-secret --storage-state configs/sessions/sc-1.json
#   rs-session totp --all --dir configs/sessions      # current code per account
#
# These variables are overrides, for anyone who would rather keep secrets in the
# environment than in a file. Per account, named after the session file
# (configs/sessions/tx-1.json -> FB_TOTP_SECRET_TX_1):
#
# FB_TOTP_SECRET_TX_1=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP
#
# Unsuffixed, for a single account — this is the one that makes sense inside a
# worker container, where the only session is /data/fb_state.json:
#
# FB_TOTP_SECRET=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP
#
# The secret is the base32 key from Settings -> Security -> Two-factor ->
# Authentication app -> "Set up another way". It is a stronger credential than
# the session cookies: cookies lapse in weeks, this mints codes until the
# account re-enrolls. Workers only need it if you configure the FB_USERNAME /
# FB_PASSWORD fallback, which is not the intended setup.

# --- Coordinator ------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy fleet-wide bearer token. Still accepted so workers deployed before
# per-worker tokens keep reporting; prefer `rsc mint-token --worker <id>`.
RS_COORDINATOR_TOKEN=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
RS_COORDINATOR_URL=http://coordinator.tailnet:8500
# Where the monitor reports each delivered alert's Telegram message_id so the
# coordinator can join partner emoji reactions back to listings (the alert
# hit-rate stats). On the homelab VM the monitor and coordinator share the
# rs-fleet docker network, so the container name resolves. Leave unset to turn
# reporting off; alerts themselves are unaffected either way.
# RS_ALERT_REPORT_URL=http://rs-coordinator:8500

# --- Control plane (coordinator host only) -----------------------------------
# These three belong ONLY on the machine running the coordinator. They are the
# keys to every Facebook account in the fleet — never copy them to a worker.
# Generate the first two with:  rsc vault init
#
# Encrypts every account secret at rest. LOSING THIS MAKES EVERY STORED SECRET
# UNREADABLE, and rotating it orphans what is already stored — back it up.
RS_VAULT_KEY=
# Per-worker bearer tokens are HMACs of this. Rotating it revokes every worker
# token at once, which is the intended emergency lever.
RS_WORKER_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Coordinator admin token. Never leaves the VM: only the coordinator and the
# rs-console facade (which uses it upstream) read it.
RS_ADMIN_TOKEN=
# What the ERP backend presents to the rs-console facade over the Cloudflare
# tunnel. Set the SAME value as COORDINATOR_API_TOKEN on the ERP backend.
# This is the only fleet credential that ever exists outside the VM.
RS_CONSOLE_TOKEN=

# Operational alerts — worker down, session expiring, challenge needing a human
# — go here, kept apart from the listing alerts so ops noise never buries a
# find. Falls back to TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID when unset.
# TELEGRAM_OPS_CHAT_ID=
#
# Fleet-wide fault dedup (README "System faults page once"). A worker reports
# the AI down after RS_INCIDENT_DOWN_AFTER straight failed evaluations and up
# after RS_INCIDENT_UP_AFTER straight successes; the coordinator pages once per
# incident key per RS_INCIDENT_COOLDOWN_HOURS and forgets a worker's `down`
# vote after RS_INCIDENT_VOTE_TTL_HOURS. Defaults shown; all optional.
# RS_INCIDENT_COOLDOWN_HOURS=6
# RS_INCIDENT_VOTE_TTL_HOURS=24
# RS_INCIDENT_DOWN_AFTER=3
# RS_INCIDENT_UP_AFTER=3

# --- Fleet agents (rs-fleetd) -------------------------------------------------
# NOT in this file. Every worker gets this file as its environment, and a host
# token reads every worker token on its host. Each Docker host keeps its agent's
# settings in configs/secrets.fleetd.env instead (gitignored, never synced):
#   RS_HOST_ID=homelab
#   RS_FLEET_ROOT=/srv/rs-fleet
#   RS_HOST_TOKEN=<output of: rsc mint-host-token --host homelab>
#   RS_FLEET_URL=http://10.10.10.73:8500   (only on a host without the coordinator)
# Host tokens are HMACs of RS_WORKER_TOKEN_SECRET too, domain-separated, so
# rotating it revokes them with the worker tokens.

# --- Worker → control plane ---------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED on every worker VM. Without both of these the worker still starts and
# still searches, but its control-plane client is never built: no heartbeat, no
# automatic 2FA, no captcha escalation, no session backup — and nothing says so
# beyond one Python warning at start-up. docker-compose.worker.yml refuses to
# start without them for exactly that reason.
#
# The token is per worker and authenticates that worker alone; the fleet-wide
# RS_COORDINATOR_TOKEN above is a separate, weaker credential for the listing
# channel and is refused outright for code and credential release.
#
# One worker per VM, so the name is the same on each node. If you must run
# several workers from a single env file, give each [worker.*] in fleet.toml a
# `control_plane_token_env = "RS_CONTROL_PLANE_TOKEN_<ID>"` and set those.
# RS_CONTROL_PLANE_URL=http://coordinator.tailnet:8500
# RS_CONTROL_PLANE_TOKEN=<output of: rsc mint-token --worker denver-1>

# --- Optional: per-region proxies -------------------------------------------
# Referenced from fleet.toml, one per worker. Match proxy geography to the
# worker's region and never point two workers at the same exit IP — one account
# per IP is what keeps the accounts alive. denver-1 is home base and uses the
# VM's own IP, so it has no entry here.
# PROXY_NORTHEAST=http://user:pass@ne-proxy.example.com:8080
# PROXY_SOUTHEAST=http://user:pass@se-proxy.example.com:8080
# PROXY_MIDWEST=http://user:pass@mw-proxy.example.com:8080
# PROXY_SOUTH_CENTRAL=http://user:pass@sc-proxy.example.com:8080
# PROXY_WEST=http://user:pass@wc-proxy.example.com:8080

# --- The homelab (phase-1) worker's proxy ------------------------------------
# Read by [monitor].proxy_server in master.toml. UNSET IS SAFE: the ${VAR}
# resolves to None with a warning and the worker keeps using the VM's own IP,
# which is exactly how it ran before 2026-08-26.
#
# ONE STICKY ENDPOINT, NOT A POOL. get_proxy_options does random.choice() over
# this value, so a rotating pool changes the exit IP mid-session — and Facebook
# binds a session to the IP it was created on, which makes rotation worse than
# no proxy. A static residential/ISP IP is the target; datacenter ASNs are the
# category Facebook distrusts most.
#
# Prefer an exit in or near DENVER. The account's history is on the house's
# Denver IP, and a sudden jump to another state is itself the kind of location
# change that gets a session challenged.
#
# After setting this, RE-CAPTURE THE SESSION THROUGH IT — a session born on the
# house IP and replayed from the proxy is a location change on an existing
# login, which is the pattern the proxy exists to avoid:
#   rs-session capture --proxy "$PROXY_HOMELAB" --storage-state <file>
# PROXY_HOMELAB=http://homelab-proxy.example.com:8080
# Only for user:pass endpoints; IP-authorised proxies leave both unset (both
# must be present or get_proxy_options ignores them).
# PROXY_HOMELAB_USER=<username>
# PROXY_HOMELAB_PASS=<password>

# --- Second homelab worker (Northeast account) --------------------------------
# rs-monitor-ne (deploy/docker-compose.homelab-ne.yml, configs/homelab-ne.toml)
# searches the Northeast on its OWN dedicated Decodo exit, so the two accounts
# on this VM never share an IP. One Decodo port = one dedicated IP:
#   10001 -> PROXY_HOMELAB    (rs-monitor,    Southeast account)
#   10002 -> PROXY_HOMELAB_NE (rs-monitor-ne, Northeast account)
#   10003 -> spare, for a third account/region
# PROXY_HOMELAB_NE=http://isp.decodo.com:10002
# PROXY_HOMELAB_NE_USER=<username>
# PROXY_HOMELAB_NE_PASS=<password>
#
# The NE worker's identity overrides live in a SEPARATE gitignored file,
# configs/secrets.homelab-ne.env (second env_file in its compose), so they do
# not collide with rs-monitor's in this shared one:
#   RS_WORKER_ID=homelab-ne
#   RS_CONTROL_PLANE_TOKEN=<rsc mint-token --worker homelab-ne>

# --- Messenger bridge and auto-reply -----------------------------------------
# rs-messenger reads the coordinator pair above (RS_COORDINATOR_URL/TOKEN) plus
# a worker identity; on a worker VM these are already set.
# RS_WORKER_ID=denver-1

# Auto-reply is OFF until this is set on the COORDINATOR. Arming it means one
# templated reply per new conversation, ever — see README "Messenger routing
# and auto-reply" for the full set of guards before switching it on.
# RS_AUTOREPLY_ENABLED=1
# RS_AUTOREPLY_MAX_PER_DAY=20
# Waking-hours window in UTC hours, start-end, wrapping midnight. 14-4 is
# roughly 8am-10pm in Denver.
# RS_AUTOREPLY_HOURS=14-4
# RS_AUTOREPLY_TEMPLATE=Thanks for reaching out! ...

# Floor between Messenger sends, seconds. Raise it if in doubt; never lower.
# RS_MESSENGER_MIN_SEND_INTERVAL=60

# --- Messenger router (mautrix-meta → Telegram forum topics) -----------------
# The router (rs-router) bridges mautrix-meta portal rooms to the coordinator,
# which opens one Telegram forum TOPIC per buyer in the war-room supergroup and
# routes staff replies back out over Messenger. See README "Messenger router".
#
# The war room is a SEPARATE Topics-enabled supergroup from the listing-alert
# chat (TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID) — enabling Topics changes the chat id, and mixing the
# two would drop buyer conversations into the middle of the finds feed. Read by
# the COORDINATOR: it sends per-buyer alerts here and long-polls it for replies.
# TELEGRAM_WARROOM_CHAT_ID=-1002000000001

# The private Matrix homeserver the bridge and router share (Conduit on the
# homelab VM). Read by rs-router.
# MATRIX_HOMESERVER_URL=http://conduit:6167
# The router bot's access token, provisioned once on the homeserver (register
# the bot user, log in, paste its token here). Read by rs-router.
# MATRIX_ROUTER_TOKEN=syt_...
# rs-router identifies to the coordinator as this worker; the transport tag
# ('matrix'), not this id, is what routes replies to it.
# RS_WORKER_ID=router
# Seconds between syncs (each sync also long-polls the homeserver).
# RS_ROUTER_INTERVAL=2
